A||||6||btoth||Yet another CD-R problem||||8||
Z||000000||btoth||11-02-2001||08:05 PM||||I can't figure this one out.<BR>I've been trying to make a CD with a collection of my favorite Cold songs.  19 tracks, just over 78 minutes long (on an 80 minute CD-R).<P>The problem:  I've made three different CDs.  The first one using Easy CD Creator 4 and an Imation CD-R.  The second time I used Easy CD Creator 5 and an Imation CD-R.  The third time I used Easy CD 5 and a Memorex CD-R.  Each one of them has a static noise when played on my car stereo and won't play at all on my parents brand new Yamaha CD player - it can never find the beginning of the tracks and acts like it's skipping.  They all play fine in my computer CD-ROM and my room stereo.<P>Things I've tried/thought:  <P>First I thought it was the software because I had been having other problems with my CD-writer not being able to read the audio CDs after it created them.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> Easy CD 5 fixed this problem, but not the problem with this CD I'm trying to make.<P>Trying different media obviously didn't fix the problem.  I made other CDs before and after these that work just fine.  All shorter though - regular CD length.<P>I thought that maybe my parents CD-player doesn't like CDs that are almost 80min long.  Not true, my old Tool collection clocks in at 79:23, and it plays perfectly. (And the Tool CD is on a Memorex 80min as well.)  And it doesn't seem to be the number of tracks - my Manson CD has 19 tracks as well and no problems.  Maybe a combination of them both?  I doubt it.<P>I was going to try making one that was only 74 minutes long and see if that worked.  But I don't want to waste a 4th CD on this until I had some opinions.  (I still might later.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>What are your thoughts?  I'm baffled.||||reg||8
Z||000001||kslish||11-03-2001||12:19 PM||||What speed are you burning the CD's at?  If you are burning at 8x or faster, try 4x or even 2x if you have the time.  (I'm still in the dark ages myself with a 4x SCSI Yamaha burner)<P>I've read some articles that say slower you burn a CD-R the more compatible and reliable it is. The theory is a slower burn (which is a physical process) will allow a more complete burn with "deeper" engraving which should in turn be more stable and more compatible with other CD readers.  This is suppossedly more important with cheap CD-R's with slow reacting dye.  <P>Also, here is the thing with 80-minute CD's.  These discs often violate standards to achieve these higher capacities, leading-to potential problems with compatibility.  74-minute CDs have a pregroove comparable to CD-Audio discs that normally use 1.4 meter-per-second tracks.  80-minute CD-Rs use 1.2 meter-per-second tracks that tightly packs the pits and lands (what the laser reads) at higher densities than is normally used.  While it is true that commercial CD's also come in 74-minute and 80-minute varieties, they also use etched aluminum as their substrate which is a lot more reflective than most dyes used in CD-R's.  If the laser doesn't get reflected back properly, the CD is going to cause problems, and 74-minute CD's just allow for a larger surface area for the CD to do it's work since the data isn't packed as close.<P>Ken S.||||reg||1
Z||000002||btoth||11-04-2001||01:55 PM||||Recording at 4x.  It's as fast as mine can go.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I had a thought:  maybe it has something to do with not enough room for the table of contents for 19 songs and 78 minutes??  Of course, the Tool CD has 13 and is 79:23 so perportionally that doesn't make sense. Hmm.  Oh well.  I decided I wasn't in the mood to burn <I>another</I> CD.<P>What you said is true though.  I always hated fast CD-ROMs.  They speed up, then slow down to actually read the data.  My cheap 33x that came with my computer, would get stuck in a loop sometimes if it kept passing up the data it wanted to read.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  My $5 4x CD-ROM works 1000 times better.  And when ever the 33x would try to read audio it would just skip because it couldn't slow down enough to read at the correct speed.||||reg||1
